<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sambhar Salt Lake</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sambhar Salt Lake is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s largest inland salt lake, about 80 km west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>. Salt from it supported the early medieval Chahamana dynasty, which tradition says was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasudeva">Vasudeva</a>, the lake's legendary discoverer, and whose homeland Shakambhari takes its name from the goddess of the lake. It remains a major source of salt and a wetland for flamingos.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The lake surrounds the historic town of Sambhar and straddles the Nagaur and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a> districts, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a> district, with the Aravali hills all around. It is a playa, a seasonal lake with a flat bottom, and the largest in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thar-desert">Thar Desert</a>. Fed mainly by four seasonal streams, the Rupangarh, Mendha, Kharian and Khandel, it drains a catchment of 5,700 square kilometres. Its water is three to four times as salty as the sea, and its depth varies from about 60 centimetres in the dry season to about 3 metres after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a>, when it covers up to 230 square kilometres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Small excavations in 1884 found terracotta pieces, coins, seals and a clay stupa, and the sculpture of Sambhar appears to show the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. A larger excavation around 1934 yielded many terracotta figurines, stoneware and decorated discs, some of them now in the Albert Hall Museum. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavata-purana">Bhagavata Purana</a> name Sambhar as the capital of the demon king Vrishparva, where his priest Shukracharya lived and his daughter Devayani married the emperor Yayati; a temple near the lake is dedicated to her.</p><p>Believers hold that the goddess Shakambhari, a form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a> and the tutelary goddess of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chauhan Rajputs</a>, created the lake: in one legend she turned a forest into a plain of precious jewels and then, fearing strife over them, into salt. The name Sambhar comes from a form of Shakambhari that arose around the 6th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal dynasty</a> managed its salt, and afterwards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a> shared it. A sandstone dam 5.1 km long divides the basin, and east of it salt has been farmed in evaporation ponds for a thousand years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The lake supplies most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>'s salt, much of it produced by the state-owned company Sambhar Salts Ltd. It was designated a Ramsar wetland in 1990 and is a wintering ground for Greater and Lesser Flamingos. In 2019 thousands of migratory birds died there of avian botulism.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sambhar Salt Lake” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tradition names Vasudeva, said to have discovered the <b>Sambhar</b> salt lake, as the dynasty's founder in the 7th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/chahamanas-of-shakambhari">Chahamanas of Shakambhari</a></li></ul></section>
